  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 304 PM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nIsolated or widely scattered showers and storms have already \ndeveloped within the sfc trough axis across western KS. Some of this \nactivity could brush parts of our central KS counties through the \nevening hours although the bulk of the activity is expected to \nremain north and west of our area of concern. A shortwave trough \nracing across the Northern Plains will drive a cold front south into \nthe area late tonight. This frontal boundary is progged to move \nsouth and east across the area on Tuesday. That will result in \nanother hot day for areas ahead of the front which includes much of \nsouthern and southeast KS where highs in the upper 90s/around 100 \nonce again. Probabilities for showers and thunderstorms will \nincrease along and behind the front on Tuesday afternoon. PWATs over \n2 inches are forecast to return to the area leading to efficient \nrainfall with any storms that develop. MLCAPE of 1500+ combined with \n30 knots of 0-6km shear may support some strong or marginally severe \nstorms although mid-lvl lapse rates appear rather marginal. This \nactivity could linger into the overnight hours as the LLJ noses into \nsouth central and southeast KS with some mid-lvl waa persisting \nacross the area. Some showers could linger over parts of central KS \nearly on Wed before the heat builds back into the area during the \nafternoon hours with above average temperatures anticipated.\n\nThe mid/upper ridge is expected to return on Thu-Fri bringing hot \nand dry conditions to the area. A shortwave trough emerging from the \nRockies will bring increasing probabilities for showers and storms \nto the area late Fri and Fri night with little relief from the heat. \nUnsettled weather conditions may linger into Sat as the upper ridge \nshifts eastward while a more robust shortwave trough moves over the \nNorthern Plains. This will eventually drive a strong cold front \nsouthward over the area late Saturday into Sunday ushering in more \nseasonable air through early next week.",
